#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184014 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"}] :question/id #uuid "10cc01a6-af19-5340-9743-ade4d5e252dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5425" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$." :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5$, and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nrank(A) + nullity(A) &= n \\\\\n3 + nullity(A) &= 5 \\\\\nnullity(A) &= 5 - 3 \\\\\ndim(Null(A)) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184015 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "78547385-d46f-5a2a-bfc1-39e040208ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5426" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n1. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n2. $A$ is invertible\n3. $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nCol(A) = R^{3}; dim(Col(A)) = 3 = rank(A); Null(A) = {0}; dim(Null(A)) = 0 = nullity(A)\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2$.Now, for the matrix $A$, the number of columns is $n = 3$, so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n$rank(A) + nullity(A)|= n; rank(A) + 2|= 3; rank(A)|= 3 - 2; = 1$.\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0$, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1$, we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184068 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcbc10c0-4e6b-5dac-ad87-f0175e4a53d2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "9615d8af-b005-5449-be4b-a7487375c98f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9963" :question/problem "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A$. If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)$?" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nrank(A) + nullity(A) &= n \\\\\n5 + nullity(A) &= 6 \\\\\nnullity(A) &= 6 - 5 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
